# Token App

<!-- 2026-08-11: adopted the Fable-orchestrator v2 layer — see
     "ORCHESTRATION LAYER" at the end of this file and .claude/ (agents,
     hooks, settings). The project brief and standing authorization below
     are unchanged. -->

A personal dashboard that syncs across the user's AI platform accounts (Claude,
ChatGPT/Codex, Gemini, Vercel, and others added over time), tracks token/usage
consumption and session/rate-limit windows, and adds a layer of intelligence
on top: maximize use of tokens already paid for, flag waste, and alert on
anomalies.

This is a single-user personal tool. There are no other users, no customer
data, and no external attack surface beyond the owner's own accounts.

## Operating mode: fully autonomous, no approval gates

The owner has explicitly authorized maximum-autonomy operation for this
project, standing instruction, valid for the life of the project unless
revoked in writing here:

- Claude may write, run, deploy, and iterate on code for this project
  **without asking for confirmation first**, including for actions that
  would normally warrant a check-in (pushing commits, deploying to
  production, changing infra config, installing dependencies, rotating or
  regenerating credentials scoped to this app).
- Claude should default to *doing*, not *proposing then waiting*. Ship,
  observe, fix forward.
- This overrides the general "ask before risky/hard-to-reverse actions"
  default for this project specifically. It does not extend to other
  projects/directories, and it does not extend to actions affecting anyone
  other than the owner (e.g. do not use this authorization to justify
  emailing someone, posting publicly, or touching shared/third-party
  infrastructure — there is none here, and it should stay that way).
- Reasonable exceptions where Claude should still pause and flag rather than
  act silently, because the blast radius is outside this app's sandbox:
  - Anything that could get the owner's platform accounts (Claude, OpenAI,
    Google, Vercel) suspended or banned — e.g. aggressive scraping that
    trips ToS enforcement. Flag the risk, let the owner decide the risk
    tolerance for that specific integration.
  - Anything that would spend real money in a surprising way (e.g.
    provisioning paid infra tiers). Cheap/free-tier infra is fine by
    default.
  - Committing secrets to git. Never do this regardless of autonomy level.
- If genuinely uncertain whether something falls under the standing
  authorization, the bar is: "would this only affect me and my own
  accounts/data, and is it reversible or cheap to fix?" If yes, proceed. If
  no, do it anyway if it's clearly what was asked for, but leave a clear
  note (commit message, changelog entry) explaining the call.

## Problem statement

Owner uses multiple AI platforms with different pricing/limit structures
(some subscription with rolling session/rate limits, some pay-per-token API
usage). Today they have no unified view of:

- How much of what they've already paid for is going unused.
- When a rate-limited session window is about to reset (so they can time
  heavy work to right after a refresh) or about to cap out (so they don't
  waste a burst of work that gets throttled mid-task).
- Whether current usage velocity is unusual vs. their own baseline.
- Whether a session/window has gone idle and refresh is being wasted.

Goal is a system that watches this automatically and tells them, rather than
them checking N different dashboards/apps.

## Step 1: Platform-by-platform technical reality check

This is the part most likely to bite us, so it's documented plainly rather
than assumed away. There are two very different categories of data:

**A. API/developer usage (metered, pay-per-token)** — generally has an
official API surface.

**B. Consumer subscription session/rate limits** (e.g. "Claude Pro resets
every 5 hours", "ChatGPT Plus weekly Codex limit") — as of this writing,
none of the major platforms expose these via a public, documented API. They
are enforced and surfaced only inside the first-party web/app clients. This
is the core blocker for the "session limit" half of the ask.

| Platform | API usage (pay-per-token) | Consumer session/rate-limit data | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude (Anthropic) | Admin API `usage_report`/cost endpoints exist for API-key/org usage. Requires an Admin API key (org-level, not present on plain Pro/Max consumer accounts). | Not exposed via API. Claude.ai Pro/Max 5-hour rolling limits are only visible in-app (Settings > Usage, and the in-conversation "you have X% left" notices). | Only path to consumer session data today is scraping the authenticated web app (fragile, ToS-sensitive) or the user manually reporting the in-app number. |
| ChatGPT / OpenAI / Codex | `platform.openai.com` usage & costs API exists for API-key usage. | ChatGPT Plus/Pro/Team message & Codex caps not exposed via public API. Visible only in-app. | Same shape of problem as Claude. Codex CLI usage (if run locally) can potentially be read from local session/log files — worth checking `~/.codex` or similar local state as a *local* data source instead of scraping. |
| Gemini | Google AI Studio / Vertex API usage is queryable via Google Cloud's billing/quota APIs for API-key-based usage. | Gemini Advanced (consumer, via Google One) limits not exposed via API. | If usage is via AI Studio API key rather than consumer subscription, this one is actually the easiest to do properly. |
| Vercel | Full REST API for usage, billing, and plan limits (`/v2/usage`, team billing endpoints), scoped by API token. | N/A — Vercel isn't session-limited the same way; it's usage/quota metered and that's exposed. | This is the one integration we can build "the right way" from day one. |
| Future platforms (as added) | Repeat this same two-column check before building an integration. | | |

**Working conclusion:** build two tiers of integration from the start rather
than pretending it's one problem:
1. **Official-API tier** (Vercel now; Claude/OpenAI/Gemini for API-key usage
   if/when the owner uses those platforms via API key rather than only the
   consumer subscription UI).
2. **Best-effort tier** for consumer session/rate-limit numbers: start with
   lightweight, low-risk methods before reaching for scraping —
   - Manual/one-tap logging (owner glances at the in-app banner, taps a
     button/shortcut to log the %, takes 2 seconds) — zero ToS risk, always
     works, good enough to start establishing baselines.
   - Local session-state files, if any platform's CLI/desktop client writes
     usage info to disk locally (check Codex CLI, Claude Code's own local
     state, etc.) — reading local files the owner already has is very
     different risk-wise from scraping a live authenticated session.
   - Only escalate to authenticated browser automation (e.g. driving Chrome
     against claude.ai / chatgpt.com to read the usage banner) if the above
     aren't sufficient, and only after flagging the ban/suspension risk
     per the exception above.

Don't build the scraper first. Build the manual-log + official-API path
first, get the intelligence layer working and useful on real data, then
layer in scraping only where it's clearly worth the account risk.

Project-owner override for Gemini/Cursor (2026-07-30): "nothing manual;
plumb it up for the API or nothing at all." Gemini API usage now has a real
Cloud Monitoring collector. Cursor has no viable individual-plan API and is
therefore listed as unavailable with no manual/fake data path.

## Step 2: information to surface

Per platform, and rolled up:

- Plan type and known limit shape (e.g. "5hr rolling window", "weekly cap",
  "pay-per-token, no cap").
- Current usage within the active window (%, tokens, or $ — whatever the
  platform gives us).
- Time remaining until window reset, and time since last reset.
- Historical usage curve per window, to establish a personal baseline
  (median tokens/hour, typical session shape) so "unusual" is defined
  relative to the owner's own behavior, not a guess.
- Utilization efficiency: windows that reset with lots of unused
  allowance ("waste"), trended over time.
- Idle detection: window open/active but no usage for N minutes given the
  owner's typical cadence.
- Cross-platform view: what's paid-for-and-unused right now, so the owner
  can route a task to the platform they're already paying for instead of
  burning a metered one.

## Step 3: alerts

Conditions to detect (from the ask, plus additions):

- Approaching limit (e.g. 80%/90% of window used).
- Burn rate substantially faster than personal baseline for this time of
  day/session type.
- New window/session just refreshed (prompt: "you have a fresh window, good
  time to batch heavy work").
- Idle allowance going unused mid-window.
- Window about to expire with significant unused allowance ("use it or lose
  it" nudge).
- Approaching a *paid overage* on a metered API platform (money-risk alert,
  higher priority than the others).
- A platform integration itself has gone stale/broken (no data in N hours) —
  meta-alert so the system doesn't fail silently.

Delivery: start with whatever's lowest-friction for a single-user local/PWA
app — push notification via the deployed web app, plus an optional
Slack/Discord/webhook or Pushover integration if the owner wants alerts to
follow them off-device. Decide exact channel when we get to Step 3
implementation; don't over-build notification infra before the data layer
is proven.

## Step 4 & 5: UI and backend

To be designed once Step 1's integration reality is confirmed against real
accounts (some of the "best-effort tier" assumptions above need to be
checked against what's actually available in 2026, not assumed from
training knowledge). Rough shape anticipated:

- Backend: a small service polling official APIs on a schedule, ingesting
  manual log taps, storing time-series usage per platform/window, running
  the baseline/anomaly logic, firing alerts.
- UI: single dashboard, one row per platform, current-window gauge,
  time-to-reset, today's/this-week's efficiency, alert feed.
- Deployable to Vercel (owner already has an account there, and it's the
  one platform with a clean official usage API to dogfood against first).

Current migration topology (2026-07-30): the Vite dashboard and Express API are
Vercel-targeted, backed by Neon Postgres through Vercel Marketplace (the
original Vercel Postgres product was retired). Collectors and the `setInterval`
scheduler remain local because they read local CLI credentials/state, and push
only normalized records/errors to the protected hosted ingest endpoint.

